pod to node relationship

how we can restrict what pods are placed on what nodes

taints and toleration have nothing to do with the scurity or intrusion on the cluster

taints and tolerations can be used to set resteiction on what pods can be scheduled on a node

lets say we have a cluster of 3 worker nodes A B C 

we have pods a,b,c,d needs to be scheduled

when the pods are created, kubernetes scheduler tries to place thes pods on the available worker nodes.
as of now there are no restrictions or limitations, the scheduler places the pods on all of them to manage the balance equally

A(a,d), B(b), C(c)
Node(pod1,pod2..)

lets us assume we have dedicated resource resource on Node A for a particular application
so we need only those pods that belongs to this application to be placed on this node A 

1)first, we prevent all pods from being places on this node A by placing a taint on the node
taint -> blue

pods should tolerate this taint to be placed in this node

by default, pods have no toleration, so unless the toleration is specified on the pods, none of them would be able to tolerate any taint on the node

2)to enable certain pods to be placed on this node
for this we must specify which pods are tolerant to this taint

if we like to allow only pod d, we add a toleration to pod d 
pod d, is now tolerant to blue
when scheduler tries to place this pod on node A, when it goes through node A, pod d can tolerate the taint blue, so it goes to node A 
Node A(pod d)


now
schdeuler tries to place pod a,b,c to node A 
since A have taint blue, these pods are not accepted by node A, so scheduler tries schedule a,b,c on diff nodes (B and C)
when scheduler tries schedule pod d, if it goes through node A, then that pod is put to A, since it tolerates the taint blue

A(d), B(a,c), C(b)

#remember
taints are set on nodes and tolerations are set on objects
taint is a key value pair 

#to put a taint on a node

kubectl taint nodes node_name key=value:taint_effect

ex: kubectl taint nodes node01 app=blue:NoSchedule

taint effect: defines what would happen to pods that do not tolerate this taint
taint-effects:
1)NoSchedule : pods will not be schedules on this node

2)PreferNoSchedule : kubernetes will try to avoid placing the pod that do not tolerate this taint, but it is not gauranteed
if no other ways available to place a pod, kubernetes will place pod

3)NoExcecute: 
NoExecute is the strictest taint effect in Kubernetes, designed to both block new pods from landing on a node and immediately evict existing pods that cannot tolerate it.


new pods will not schedules on the node and existing pods on the node, if any will be evicted if they do not tolerate the taint
these pods may have been scheduled on the node before taint was applied to the node 

