tolerations are added to the pods

to add the toleration  to the pod:

1) add it in pod definition file
$under spec section add a section called  tolerations
#use the same values used while creating taint in node
#all the values should be enclosed by double quotes
#you can add multiple tolerations to a pod so that a pod can scheduled into any tolerated nodes

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata: 
  name: my-app
  labels: 
    app: myapp
    type: frontend
spec: 
  containers: 
    - name: nginx
      image: nginx
  tolerations:
    - key: "app"
      operator: "Equal"
      value: "blue"
      effect: NoSchedule
    #means key=app:NoSchedule

You can apply a taint to a node with specialized GPU hardware so regular workloads do not take up space.
kubectl taint nodes gpu-node-1 hardware=gpu:NoSchedule

Add a toleration to your GPU-heavy pod's YAML manifest
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: ml-training-pod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: trainer
    image: tensorflow/tensorflow:latest
  tolerations:
  - key: "hardware"
    operator: "Equal"
    value: "gpu"
    effect: "NoSchedule"


taints and tolerations are only meant to restrict nodes from accepting certain pods

but it does not gaurantee that  a pod with certain toleration will be scheduled to only to those it which taint matches or to a particular node where its taints matches the pods toleration

it only tell nodes to accpt only these pods

1)a pod can also be scheduled to other nodes where there is no taint
2)a pod can also be scheduled to other nodes where there its taint matches the tolerations(multiple tolerations of pod) or if multiple nodes have same taint
